 "issue": 1903,
 "issue_title": "Bug: reject unsupported object scalars in from_pandas",
 "issue_body": "## Summary\n\n`from_pandas()` still silently converts unsupported non-primitive object-dtype scalar values with `str(value)`. The earlier unsupported-scalar fix now rejects `Timestamp`, `Timedelta`, and complex values, but arbitrary objects and several common Python scalar-like values still pass through as strings without an explicit user decision.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nSilent stringification can corrupt data before it reaches downstream pandas, Arrow, schema validation, or model-training workflows. A data cleaning library should either support a scalar type intentionally or reject it with a clear conversion hint.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `arnio/convert.py`: `_normalize_scalar()` falls back to `str(value)` for any value that is not `bool`, `int`, or `str` after the special-case checks.\n- `arnio/convert.py`: `_series_to_python_values()` rejects a few unsupported object scalars, but not arbitrary custom objects, `bytes`, `datetime.date`, `datetime.time`, or `pandas.Period` values.\n- `tests/test_convert.py`: the regression coverage for unsupported object scalars focuses on `Timestamp`, `Timedelta`, and complex values, so this broader fallback remains uncovered.\n\n## Reproduction or trigger\n\n1. Run this on current `main`:\n\n```python\nimport datetime as dt\nimport pandas as pd\nimport arnio as ar\n\nclass Token:\n    def __str__(self):\n        return \"TOKEN\"\n\ncases = [\n    Token(),\n    b\"abc\",\n    dt.date(2026, 5, 29),\n    dt.time(12, 30),\n    pd.Period(\"2026-05\"),\n]\n\nfor value in cases:\n    df = pd.DataFrame({\"x\": pd.Series([value], dtype=object)})\n    frame = ar.from_pandas(df)\n    print(type(value).__name__, ar.to_pandas(frame).to_dict(\"list\"))\n```\n\n2. The values are accepted and stringified.\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nUnsupported object-dtype scalar values should raise a clear `TypeError` that includes the column name, the unsupported Python type, and a suggested conversion path. Explicitly supported types should be documented and tested.\n\n## Actual behavior\n\nUnsupported object scalar values are converted to strings, for example `Token()` becomes `\"TOKEN\"` and `b\"abc\"` becomes `\"b'abc'\"`.\n\n## Suggested direction\n\n- Replace the broad `str(value)` fallback with an explicit allowlist for supported scalar types.\n- Consider whether `datetime.date` / `datetime.time` should be rejected with a conversion hint or intentionally supported as ISO strings.\n- Add regression tests for custom objects, `bytes`, date/time objects, and `pandas.Period` inside object-dtype columns.\n- Keep plain object string columns and documented mixed primitive behavior unchanged.\n\n## References\n\n- Follow-up to the narrower unsupported-scalar work in #716.\r\n",
 "issue_url": "https://github.com/im-anishraj/arnio/issues/1903",
 "pr": 1983,
 "pr_title": "fix: reject unsupported object scalars in from_pandas",
 "pr_body": "## Summary\r\nReplaced the overly broad `str(value)` fallback mechanism in `from_pandas` scalar normalization with a strict primitive type allowlist. Now, instead of silently stringifying and potentially corrupting unsupported object-dtype scalars (like arbitrary custom objects, `bytes`, `datetime.date`, `datetime.time`, or `pd.Period`), arnio safely raises a descriptive `TypeError` containing the column name, the invalid type, and a suggestion path for the user.\r\n\r\n## Linked issue\r\nFixes #1903\r\n\r\n## Type of change\r\n- [x] Bug fix\r\n- [ ] Feature\r\n- [ ] Performance improvement\r\n- [ ] Documentation\r\n- [x] Tests\r\n- [ ] Refactor\r\n- [ ] CI / packaging\r\n\r\n## Area\r\n- [ ] CSV parser / I/O\r\n- [ ] C++ cleaning engine\r\n- [ ] Python API / ArFrame\r\n- [ ] Pipeline / custom steps\r\n- [ ] Data quality / profiling\r\n- [ ] Schema validation\r\n- [x] pandas interoperability\r\n- [ ] Docs / website\r\n- [ ] Developer tooling\r\n\r\n## Testing\r\n- [x] `make test` (2429 passed including:\r\n  - `test_from_pandas_object_custom_class_raises_clear_error`\r\n  - `test_from_pandas_object_bytes_raises_clear_error`\r\n  - `test_from_pandas_object_datetime_date_and_time_raise_clear_error`\r\n  - `test_from_pandas_object_pandas_period_raises_clear_error`)\r\n- [x] `make lint`\r\n\r\n## Screenshots / Media\r\n\r\n## Performance Impact\r\n\r\n## GSSoC labels\r\n\r\n- `level:intermediate`\r\n- `type:bug`\r\n-  `area:pandas-interop`\r\n\r\n## Contributor checklist\r\n\r\n* [x] I read the contributing guide.\r\n* [x] I kept the PR focused on one issue or one logical change.\r\n* [x] I added or updated tests for behavior changes.\r\n* [x] I added or updated docs/examples for public API changes.\r\n* [x] I checked that generated files, logs, and local build artifacts are not committed.\r\n* [x] My PR title uses Conventional Commits (`feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `test:`, `refactor:`, `chore:`).\r\n\r\n## Maintainer notes",
